		<dc:title id="title">An Enquiry Concerning the Principles of Morals</dc:title>
		<meta property="file-as" refines="#title">Enquiry Concerning the Principles of Morals, An</meta>
		<dc:subject id="subject-1">Ethics</dc:subject>
		<meta property="authority" refines="#subject-1">LCSH</meta>
		<meta property="term" refines="#subject-1">sh00005644</meta>
		<meta property="schema:genre">Nonfiction</meta>
		<meta property="schema:genre">Philosophy</meta>
		<meta property="schema:abstract">David Hume argues that the general principles of morality are to be understood not in terms of reason but rather in terms of the “sentiment of humanity.”</meta>
		<dc:description>
			&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/david-hume"&gt;David Hume&lt;/a&gt; initially published &lt;i&gt;The Enquiry Concerning the Principles of Morals&lt;/i&gt; in 1751, following the publication of his &lt;a href="https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/david-hume/a-treatise-of-human-nature"&gt;&lt;i&gt;Treatise of Human Nature&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; in 1739–40 and his &lt;a href="https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/david-hume/an-enquiry-concerning-human-understanding"&gt;&lt;i&gt;Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; in 1748. In these earlier works, he argued that our ability to reason is limited to matters of fact and “relations of ideas” (roughly how our concepts connect to each other). The implication is that reason cannot on its own help us discover what is good or valuable. Rather, values in general derive from our “&lt;em&gt;passions&lt;/em&gt;”: our perceptions of pleasure and pain that motivate us to act. Consequently, in &lt;i&gt;The Enquiry Concerning the Principles of Morals&lt;/i&gt;, which he ultimately assessed to be “incomparably the best” of his work, Hume offers an account of the general principles of morality in terms of the passions and our “&lt;em&gt;sympathy&lt;/em&gt;,” which he understands to be our tendency to feel the passions of others.&lt;/p&gt;
			&lt;p&gt;The theory Hume develops is a kind of &lt;em&gt;virtue ethics&lt;/em&gt;, finding our assessments of each other as having various virtues or vices as fundamental to our moral evaluations quite generally. When someone displays a character that makes them useful or agreeable (or their opposites) to themself or to others, Hume argues, we naturally feel sentiments of pleasure (or pain) or of approval (or disapproval)—sentiments grounded in our sympathy with those affected by their conduct. When one has such sympathy in response to another’s character regardless of the relation to oneself either of them or of those affected by them, one’s sentiment is the “&lt;em&gt;sentiment of humanity&lt;/em&gt;,” and the resulting evaluations are properly &lt;em&gt;moral&lt;/em&gt; evaluations both of those characters as manifesting virtues or vices and of actions arising out of those characters as right or wrong.&lt;/p&gt;
			&lt;p&gt;This work is striking and original, grounded in astute observations of our moral psychology. It has had an enormous and lasting legacy on philosophical moral thought from its publication through the present day.&lt;/p&gt;
		</dc:description>
